* dsPIC33EPXXGS202 Family Pin Definitions -- 28-pin SOIC/SSOP/QFN-S/UQFN
* packages (all share the identical 28-signal set; only physical pin
* NUMBERING differs between the SOIC/SSOP diagram and the QFN-style
* diagrams in the datasheet -- irrelevant to this file's records, since
* none of them encode physical pin numbers. See DS70005208E page 3-4 if
* the numbering difference itself ever matters for a physical layout.)
* Source: Microchip DS70005208E "dsPIC33EPXXGS202 Family Data Sheet",
*         Table 1-1 (Pinout I/O Descriptions) and the 28-Pin SOIC/SSOP
*         Pin Diagram (page 3). Extracted directly from the vendor PDF's
*         text layer -- not reconstructed from search snippets, unlike
*         the PIC16F819 file -- so no separate sourcing-reliability
*         caveat is needed here.
*
* Flagged for manual review (dual-function / non-obvious pins):
*   RB2/OSC2/CLKO/AN7/PGA1N2/RP34 -- Table 1-1's own Note 2: at device
*                     power-up, a glitch pulse (~2V amplitude, >500us
*                     duration) may appear on this pin regardless of
*                     pull-down resistors. Datasheet explicitly
*                     recommends NOT using this pin as an output driver
*                     unless the circuit it drives can tolerate that.
*   AVDD, AVSS      -- separate ANALOG power rail, not the same net as
*                     VDD/VSS (Table 1-1: "must be connected at all
*                     times", distinct Pin Type "P" entries from VDD/
*                     VSS). The qpindef format only supports one V and
*                     one G record, so these don't fit either -- recorded
*                     as I (input), matching the VCAP treatment below.
*                     This is an inference extending an explicit
*                     instruction given for VCAP specifically, not
*                     something independently confirmed as the intended
*                     handling for AVDD/AVSS -- flagging for review.
*   VCAP            -- CPU logic filter capacitor connection (Table 1-1
*                     Pin Type: P). NOT simply a passive external
*                     decoupling point -- it's the output of the on-chip
*                     1.8V core-logic regulator that the CPU core
*                     actually runs from, and it's actively monitored
*                     internally by the Brown-out Reset circuit. Recorded
*                     as I (input) per explicit instruction, despite this
*                     not being a precise electrical match -- the format
*                     has no dedicated category for an internal-regulator
*                     node with no VDD/VSS/GPIO equivalent.
*   MCLR            -- dedicated Reset input (Table 1-1 Pin Type I/P).
*                     Unlike PIC16F819, this pin is NOT multiplexed with
*                     any GPIO port bit on this device -- no RAx/RBx
*                     alternate function exists for it at all.
*
* Every RAx/RBx pin's Peripheral Pin Select (PPS)-remappable alternate
* functions (the RPnn-numbered names) are software-configurable at
* runtime, not fixed like PIC16F819's dedicated alternate names -- this
* does not affect classification (the underlying GPIO bit is
* bidirectional regardless of what's remapped onto it via PPS), but
* means the alt-text field below is busier per pin (5-6 alternate names
* is typical) than on simpler fixed-mux devices.
*
* VSS occupies two physical pins (both are the same net) on this
* package; still one G record. VDD, AVDD, AVSS, VCAP, and MCLR each
* occupy exactly one physical pin.

D dsPIC33EPXXGS202 Family 16-Bit Digital Signal Controllers for Digital Power Applications with Interconnected High-Speed PWM, ADC, PGA and Comparators
P dsPIC33EP16GS202 dsPIC33EP32GS202

